questions.answer:
async function retry(fn, attempts = 3, delay = 100) {
  let lastError;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      lastError = err;
      if (i < attempts - 1) {
        await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, delay));
      }
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}

let calls = 0;
retry(async () => {
  calls++;
  if (calls < 3) throw new Error("flaky " + calls);
  return "Kakade";
}, 3, 50).then((value) => console.log(value, calls));
// Kakade 3

The loop counts attempts, not retries: three attempts means at most two waits. await fn() inside try catches both a synchronous throw and a rejected Promise. Returning immediately on success skips the remaining attempts. After the final failure the loop exits and throws lastError, so the caller still sees a rejection. A production version adds exponential backoff and only retries transient errors such as 429 or 503, never a 400.

